<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1527">1527</a>–<a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maximilian II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>, reigned from 1564 to 1576. Sympathetic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, he granted religious concessions to the Austrian nobility and sought peace within the Empire, while fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. In 1574 he raised the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> to a duchy for the Gonzaga of Mantua.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Nicolas Neufchatel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_Neufch%C3%A2tel_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maximilian was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 31 July 1527, the eldest son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> king Ferdinand I and nephew of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. He was educated mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by humanist scholars, learned something of Lutheran teaching in his youth and corresponded early with the Protestant prince Augustus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. On 13 September 1548 he married his cousin Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the marriage produced sixteen children, of whom nine lived to adulthood.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> he grew close to the court preacher Sebastian Pfauser, which alarmed his father. After several refusals he agreed in 1560 to Pfauser's banishment and attended Catholic Mass again, though his views were tinged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> to the end of his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Maximilian was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a> in 1562, elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> on 24 November 1562 and crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in 1563. He succeeded Ferdinand as emperor on 25 July 1564. He granted religious liberty to the Lutheran nobles and knights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and refused to publish the decrees of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>, but he would not meet the demands of the Lutheran princes at the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1566. His reign saw the high point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, which he did not try to suppress.</p><p>War with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> resumed in 1566, and they took Szigetvár while their sultan died during the siege. The Treaty of Adrianople of 1568 required Maximilian to recognise Ottoman suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, Wallachia and Moldavia. In 1576 part of the Polish and Lithuanian magnates elected him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory">Stephen Báthory</a>, but he was not widely accepted there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Maximilian died at Regensburg on 12 October 1576 while preparing to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and on his deathbed he refused the last sacraments. He is buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St Vitus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. His eldest surviving son Rudolf succeeded him. His court was a centre of humanist scholarship where Catholic and Protestant scholars worked together, including the botanist Carolus Clusius, and his library under Hugo Blotius became the nucleus of the Austrian National Library. Paula Sutter Fichtner holds that he failed in his three main aims, while Peter Marshall argues that his tolerance and patronage kept a precarious peace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Emperor initially objected that only he could grant such a title, but in 1576 <b>Maximilian II</b> recognised it for Cosimo’s son Francesco I, completing the creation of a sovereign state that would last for almost three centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1574 <b>Emperor Maximilian II</b> raised it to a duchy for Duke Guglielmo Gonzaga, who governed it from Mantua through a council at Casale.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-montferrat">Duchy of Montferrat</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1574 <b>Emperor Maximilian II</b> raised it to a duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-montferrat">March of Montferrat</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Regensburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1558" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4231 5922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4231 5922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1558">What was here in 1558? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
